American crows—Corvus brachyrhynchos—can remember human faces for years and will scold the same person who once threatened them. Your puppy’s brain is still smaller than a crow’s, so keep training sessions short. Use tiny pieces of low-fat boiled chicken or plain kibble from her regular bowl; that keeps calories low and avoids the raw-food risks your vet warned about.  

How many treats? Think crow-sized snacks: about the size of a peanut for every five minutes of good behavior. Crows cache thousands of food bits and still find them months later, so if you hide a few treats in the house she’ll stay busy and you’ll stay under the calorie ceiling.  

If she seems bored, give her a puzzle feeder or a slow-feeder bowl;

Congrats on Cooper! Labs at three months are mostly curiosity and tail-wags, so keeping the meals steady and the snacks strategic really helps.

First, food brands matter less than the first four lines on the label. Look for the AAFCO “complete and balanced for growth” claim and 28–30% protein from named sources like chicken or lamb. Good starting options: Royal Canin Labrador Retriever Puppy, Purina Pro Plan Puppy, or Hill’s Science Diet Puppy. These are kibble, no surprises.

Feeding schedule: three small meals a day, same times each day. At 3 months, aim for about 1½ cups total, split ½–½–½. As he hits 5 months, drop to two meals;
